#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf13639-8d18-5909-a26d-aeda1e011ac0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Without Simplification"}] :question/id #uuid "75ac9866-6b0f-5736-a592-0bade16b8814" :question/math-academy-id "e-2333" :question/problem "Winston drove $200$ kilometers in two days. If he drove $110$ kilometers on the first day, what percentage of the total distance did he drive on the first day?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case,\n\n- the part is the number of kilometers Winston drove the first day, which is $110,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of kilometers he drove, which is $200.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 110 \\div 200 = \\dfrac{110}{200}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{110}{200} = \\dfrac{110 \\div 2}{200 \\div 2} = \\dfrac{55}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{55}{100} = 55 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, Winston drove $55 \\%$ of the total distance on the first day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f67408f-f019-5365-b4a0-6f60ac23cc1d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Using Simplification"}] :question/id #uuid "2f3270cc-d191-5282-b9f7-cf63dcd69d70" :question/math-academy-id "e-2334" :question/problem "In his previous youth baseball season, Matt hit $75$ extra-bases, $9$ of which were home runs. What percentage of extra-base hits were home runs?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of home runs, which is $9,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of extra-base hits Matt connected, which is $75.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage} = 9 \\div 75 = \\dfrac{9}{75}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{75}=\\dfrac{9 \\div 3}{75 \\div 3}=\\dfrac{3}{25}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{25} = \\dfrac{3 \\times 4}{25 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{12}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{100} = 12 \\%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12 \\%$ of extra-base hits were home runs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf13639-8d18-5909-a26d-aeda1e011ac0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Without Simplification"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b5741df-80a6-5873-8f04-d0e69effa41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458676} {:answer/id #uuid "3732cf4b-7032-5a08-a2ae-523eea2936a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458735} {:answer/id #uuid "afaae9fc-572f-5d6a-a0fb-c3328f842a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458940} {:answer/id #uuid "e59c5448-f727-5f75-b9c4-19669e8914c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459027} {:answer/id #uuid "ec247bd1-9d21-5cb2-8c16-c45e83cff7fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afaae9fc-572f-5d6a-a0fb-c3328f842a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458940} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb486c6b-701a-5219-b492-47c6cfbdb546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80d2fda3-c051-5035-ae22-72b7e72df2b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-147021" :question/problem "Ashley is watching a TV series with $50$ episodes. So far, she has watched $8$ episodes. What percentage of the TV series has Ashley watched?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of episodes Ashley has watched, which is $8,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of episodes, which is $50.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=8÷50=\\frac{8}{50}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{50}=\\frac{8\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{16}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{100}=16%\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ashley has watched $16%$ of the TV series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f67408f-f019-5365-b4a0-6f60ac23cc1d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Using Simplification"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205b8b00-0213-5e30-b415-2055e4c4dd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458689} {:answer/id #uuid "2aaaf21f-eb95-57c0-af53-78ab32712771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458713} {:answer/id #uuid "4d83be22-ce90-53a2-8fc7-684eb63b38c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458768} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5d8b56-1d26-5a84-8290-232659c48665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458965} {:answer/id #uuid "ffedf323-2f2f-5902-be03-b70e53b27538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba5d8b56-1d26-5a84-8290-232659c48665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458965} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e2d972-7257-5252-93de-1eebd94a58c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e7deab4b-7eef-5f1d-8c28-150c55d5122c" :question/math-academy-id "q-147035" :question/problem "There are $52$ cards and $13$ clubs in a poker deck. What percentage of the deck are clubs?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of clubs in the deck, which is $13,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of cards in the deck, which is $52.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=13÷52=\\frac{13}{52}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{13}{52}=\\frac{13÷13}{52÷13}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}=25%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $25%$ of the poker deck are clubs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf13639-8d18-5909-a26d-aeda1e011ac0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Without Simplification"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3232bd3-359b-53ab-952e-6fe00f2e3427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178985} {:answer/id #uuid "c88f4b65-d203-5e9b-91b8-0b6e3f72c021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178986} {:answer/id #uuid "122f2591-2572-58fe-ad18-214dfc688121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178987} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc9d0c7-9fe5-5e91-a6bc-d8a400aa2938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178988} {:answer/id #uuid "7536fd18-fd67-5468-8f24-8ec13351d149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7536fd18-fd67-5468-8f24-8ec13351d149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178989} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebc62738-68e7-57ca-a8d2-506d09b09bb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e688fddb-8c8a-5d00-8ff1-44d42499d959" :question/math-academy-id "q-60919" :question/problem "In a supermarket, $16$ out of $200$ watermelons were unripe. What percentage of watermelons were unripe?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of unripe watermelons, which is $16,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of watermelons, which is $200.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=16÷200=\\frac{16}{200}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{200}=\\frac{16÷2}{200÷2}=\\frac{8}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{100}=8%\n$$\n\nTherefore, $8%$ of the watermelons were unripe."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf13639-8d18-5909-a26d-aeda1e011ac0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Without Simplification"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33e02cd8-76ea-56bf-be11-5f0c1eaf821a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458729} {:answer/id #uuid "6b61940c-b90b-5e0d-bf86-e66846adfd2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458817} {:answer/id #uuid "6fca0c7f-964b-5c6f-9bdd-5cc809a36efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458823} {:answer/id #uuid "72ed0da1-b2bd-5495-9858-33ebc96fdf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458828} {:answer/id #uuid "92cf3ebe-9ba5-5ef6-b89a-7e9b43c79e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92cf3ebe-9ba5-5ef6-b89a-7e9b43c79e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458885} :answer-field/id #uuid "579fcabb-9f51-5ad2-89db-28010c6fd50f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ada40db-1757-59fe-9612-8be9b0df34b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-93122" :question/problem "David saved $$500$ to buy a new cellphone. If he spent only $$420$ to buy the cellphone, what percentage of David's savings did he spend on the phone?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the money David spent, which is $420,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total amount of money saved, which is $500.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=420÷500=\\frac{42}{50}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{42}{50}=\\frac{42\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{84}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{84}{100}=84%\n$$\n\nTherefore, David spent $84%$ of the total money saved."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f67408f-f019-5365-b4a0-6f60ac23cc1d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Percent Given Two Numbers Using Simplification"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "258b396e-193a-5152-ab71-65790f071bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458704} {:answer/id #uuid "67786787-3341-5686-8d81-9fccf9af01a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458811} {:answer/id #uuid "6c980d8c-7cd5-5911-892f-f92121a7f872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458819} {:answer/id #uuid "b94bb37f-f961-5fa0-99ef-3d1732458f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458962} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c0a062-fc38-5837-af40-23d1b71cba19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c980d8c-7cd5-5911-892f-f92121a7f872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458819} :answer-field/id #uuid "77a90351-e491-5706-b578-ae7f10a99eed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb35eeb2-cf93-5ec3-9596-7f82799783b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-93128" :question/problem "On average, George exercises for $120$ minutes each day. Of this time, he spends $54$ minutes running. What percentage of his exercise time does George spend running?" :question/worked-solution "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the number of minutes George runs, which is $54,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total number of minutes George exercises, which is $120.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=54÷120=\\frac{54}{120}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{54}{120}=\\frac{54÷6}{120÷6}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=45%\n$$\n\nTherefore, George runs for $45%$ of his overall training time."}]}
